## DESCRIPTION
## Find the minimum distance from a point to a line
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Inner products)
## DBsection(Projection and distance)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## Level(1)
## KEYWORDS('projection', 'point', 'line')

# updated 2022.12.12

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Vector');

# Define all variables used in the problem statement and solution

$u = ColumnVector(non_zero_point2D(2, 7));
@upt = $u->value;
$v = ColumnVector(non_zero_point2D(1, 4));


Context('Fraction');
$proj = Fraction($u . $v, $v . $v) * $v;
Context('Vector');
$w    = $u - $proj;
$d = norm($w);

BEGIN_PGML

Find the minimum distance from the point [` ([$upt[0]],[$upt[1]]) `] to the line
[` L = \left\{ [$v] t  \; | \; t \in \mathbb{R} \right\} `]
using the following steps:

1. Let [`\vec{u}`] be the vector from the origin to the point and [`\vec{v}`] be the
    vector parallel to the line.  
		[`\text{Proj}_{\vec{v}} (\vec{u}) `] = [____]{$proj}

2. [` \vec{u} - \text{Proj}_{\vec{v}} (\vec{u}) = `] [____]{$w}

3. The minimum distance is the value [` ||\vec{u} - \text{Proj}_{\vec{v}} (\vec{u}) || = `] [_____]{$d}

For 1. and 2. your answer should be a vector in [`\mathbb{R}^2 `], like "<1,2>".
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
1. The projection onto the line is the same as the projection onto the vector
		[`\vec{v}`] or [``\text{Proj}_{\vec{v}}(\vec{v}) = \frac{\vec{u} \cdot
		\vec{v}}{||\vec{v}||^2}\vec{v} = \frac{ [@ $u . $v @]}{[@ $v . $v @]} [$v]
		= [$proj] ``]

2. [` \vec{u} - \text{Proj}_L (\vec{u}) = [$w]`]

3. The minimum distance is
		[` ||\vec{u} - \text{Proj}_L (\vec{u}) || = \sqrt{[@ $w . $w @]} = [$d]`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
